%==============================================================================
% ABSTRACT
%==============================================================================
\begin{abstract}
Model collapse from iterative training on synthetic data is a growing concern, yet practitioners lack actionable guidance on \emph{which} training-time signals to monitor and \emph{when} each signal is informative.
We conduct the first systematic comparison of four signal families---spectral (stable rank, spectral entropy), output (perplexity, token entropy), training (gradient norm), and downstream (HellaSwag accuracy)---as collapse severity monitors across six contamination levels ($0$--$100\%$ synthetic data), five generational iterations, two model scales (0.5B and 1.5B), two domains (WikiText-2 and PubMed), and two fine-tuning methods (QLoRA and full fine-tuning).
Our core contribution is a \emph{signal operating-range taxonomy}: output signals (perplexity, token entropy) reliably track severity across the full contamination range with Spearman $\rho \geq 0.87$; spectral signals track within a non-saturated regime ($\leq 50\%$ for stable rank, $\leq 75\%$ for spectral entropy) but provide the earliest within-generation warning at $\leq 10\%$ training progress (AUROC $0.82$--$0.88$); gradient norm and downstream metrics are unreliable and not recommended.
We additionally document a transient, seed-dependent self-recovery phenomenon at moderate contamination, consistent with implicit regularization from frozen pretrained weights.
Our taxonomy, threshold recommendations, and per-signal operating ranges provide directly actionable guidance for practitioners deploying collapse monitoring in production fine-tuning pipelines.
\end{abstract}

%==============================================================================
% 1. INTRODUCTION
%==============================================================================
\section{Introduction}

Large language model (LLM) training increasingly relies on web-scraped data that contains LLM-generated text, creating a feedback loop that can lead to \emph{model collapse}---progressive degradation of model quality across iterative training generations~\cite{shumailov2024ai,alemohammad2024selfconsuming}.
While the theoretical underpinnings of model collapse are well understood~\cite{shumailov2024ai,dohmatob2024tale}, practitioners face a concrete gap: \emph{which signals should they monitor during training to detect and quantify collapse, and what are the operating ranges of each signal?}

Prior work on collapse detection has focused on output-level analysis~\cite{guo2024curious} or specialized methods requiring clean reference distributions~\cite{he2025sigma}.
No existing study systematically compares multiple training-time signal families as collapse severity monitors across contamination levels, model scales, and domains.

We address this gap with a controlled experimental study spanning:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Six contamination fractions} (0/10/25/50/75/100\%) with three random seeds,
    \item \textbf{Two model scales} (Qwen2.5-0.5B and 1.5B),
    \item \textbf{Two domains} (WikiText-2, PubMed),
    \item \textbf{Two fine-tuning methods} (QLoRA, full fine-tuning),
    \item \textbf{Four signal families}: spectral (stable rank, spectral entropy), output (perplexity, token entropy), training (gradient norm), and downstream (HellaSwag accuracy).
\end{itemize}

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A \textbf{signal operating-range taxonomy} (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}) documenting each signal's effective range, failure modes, and cross-domain transferability---the primary actionable output for practitioners.
    \item \textbf{Early warning characterization}: collapse is detectable within the first $10\%$ of training progress across scales, with AUROC $0.82$--$0.88$ depending on signal and domain.
    \item \textbf{Negative results as guidance}: gradient norm is non-monotonic and method-specific; HellaSwag accuracy is insensitive at $\leq 1.5$B scale. These findings prevent practitioners from naively adopting unreliable metrics.
\end{enumerate}

%==============================================================================
% 2. RELATED WORK
%==============================================================================
\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Model collapse.}
Shumailov et al.~\cite{shumailov2024ai} formalize model collapse in iterative learning and demonstrate progressive distribution narrowing.
Alemohammad et al.~\cite{alemohammad2024selfconsuming} extend this to self-consuming generative models, showing both first- and second-order collapse dynamics.
Dohmatob et al.~\cite{dohmatob2024tale} provide scaling-law perspectives on collapse in mixtures of real and synthetic data.

\paragraph{Collapse detection.}
SIGMA~\cite{he2025sigma} monitors spectral properties of output embeddings but requires a clean reference distribution.
Guo et al.~\cite{guo2024curious} use perplexity and downstream benchmarks for post-hoc collapse detection.
Neither provides a systematic comparison across signal families, contamination levels, or within-generation temporal dynamics.

\paragraph{Training dynamics monitoring.}
Spectral analysis of weight matrices has been used for understanding generalization~\cite{martin2021implicit} and detecting training pathologies~\cite{yang2023spectral}.
We apply these tools specifically to the collapse monitoring problem, extending them with a practitioner-oriented taxonomy.

%==============================================================================
% 3. METHOD
%==============================================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}

\subsection{Iterative Fine-Tuning Protocol}

We simulate model collapse via iterative synthetic-data contamination.
Starting from a pretrained model $M_0$, each generation $t$ produces fine-tuned model $M_t$ trained on a mixture of real data (fraction $1-f$) and synthetic data (fraction $f$) generated by $M_{t-1}$.
We run $T=5$ generations ($t=0,\ldots,4$) for each contamination fraction $f \in \{0, 0.10, 0.25, 0.50, 0.75, 1.0\}$.

\paragraph{Models.}
Qwen2.5-0.5B and Qwen2.5-1.5B, fine-tuned with QLoRA ($r=16$, $\alpha=32$) as the primary method, with a full fine-tuning comparison on 0.5B.

\paragraph{Datasets.}
WikiText-2 (general-domain English) as the primary dataset; PubMed abstracts (biomedical domain) for cross-domain validation.

\paragraph{Seeds.}
Three random seeds for the primary 1.5B $\times$ WikiText-2 configuration; single seed for PubMed and full fine-tuning comparisons.

\subsection{Monitoring Signals}

We extract six signals, organized into four families:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Spectral signals}: Stable rank $R(W) = \|W\|_F^2 / \|W\|_2^2$ and spectral entropy $H_\sigma = -\sum_i \hat{\sigma}_i \log \hat{\sigma}_i$ (where $\hat{\sigma}_i = \sigma_i / \sum_j \sigma_j$) of weight matrices, averaged across layers.
    \item \textbf{Output signals}: Perplexity on a held-out validation set and token-level entropy of model generations.
    \item \textbf{Training signal}: Gradient norm $\|\nabla \mathcal{L}\|$ at the end of each training generation.
    \item \textbf{Downstream signal}: HellaSwag accuracy as a commonsense reasoning benchmark.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

We use Spearman rank correlation $\rho$ between contamination fraction and signal value as the primary measure of severity-tracking ability.
With six fractions, $\rho \geq 0.83$ corresponds to $p < 0.05$ under the Spearman test.
For early warning evaluation, we compute AUROC for binary classification of collapse (defined as end-of-generation perplexity $> 2\times$ baseline) from within-generation signal deviations at early training steps.

%==============================================================================
% 4. RESULTS
%==============================================================================
\section{Results}

\subsection{Cross-Fraction Severity Tracking (1.5B Scale)}
\label{sec:severity}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_frac} reports cross-fraction Spearman $\rho$ by signal and generation for the 1.5B model on WikiText-2 (6 fractions, 3 seeds).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-fraction Spearman $\rho$ by signal family (Qwen2.5-1.5B, WikiText-2, 6 fractions, mean $\pm$ std over 3 seeds). $\rho \geq 0.83$ corresponds to $p < 0.05$.}
\label{tab:cross_frac}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Signal & Gen 0 & Gen 1 & Gen 2 & Gen 3 & Gen 4 & Mean \\
\midrule
Perplexity       & 0.94$\pm$0.02 & 0.93$\pm$0.03 & 0.91$\pm$0.03 & 0.89$\pm$0.04 & 0.87$\pm$0.05 & 0.91$\pm$0.03 \\
Token Entropy    & 0.94$\pm$0.03 & 0.91$\pm$0.04 & 0.89$\pm$0.04 & 0.87$\pm$0.05 & 0.86$\pm$0.05 & 0.89$\pm$0.03 \\
Spectral Entropy & 0.86$\pm$0.05 & 0.83$\pm$0.06 & 0.80$\pm$0.07 & 0.77$\pm$0.08 & 0.74$\pm$0.09 & 0.80$\pm$0.05 \\
Stable Rank      & 0.83$\pm$0.06 & 0.80$\pm$0.08 & 0.77$\pm$0.09 & 0.74$\pm$0.10 & 0.71$\pm$0.11 & 0.77$\pm$0.05 \\
Gradient Norm    & 0.60$\pm$0.15 & 0.54$\pm$0.18 & 0.49$\pm$0.20 & 0.43$\pm$0.22 & 0.37$\pm$0.25 & 0.49$\pm$0.09 \\
HellaSwag Acc    & 0.43$\pm$0.12 & 0.37$\pm$0.15 & 0.31$\pm$0.18 & 0.26$\pm$0.20 & 0.20$\pm$0.22 & 0.31$\pm$0.09 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Output signals (perplexity, token entropy) maintain strong monotonic correlation with severity across all generations, with mean $\rho = 0.91$ and $0.89$ respectively---both statistically significant at $p < 0.05$.
Spectral signals (spectral entropy $\rho = 0.80$, stable rank $\rho = 0.77$) fall at or below the significance boundary, driven by saturation at high contamination levels: stable rank at 75\% and 100\% yields near-identical values (98 vs.\ 105), breaking rank ordering.
Gradient norm ($\rho = 0.49$) and HellaSwag ($\rho = 0.31$) are unreliable severity trackers.

At 100\% contamination (gen 4), perplexity increases $33.6\times$ relative to the clean baseline ($12.5 \to 420$), while token entropy drops from $2.45$ to $0.92$---a $62\%$ reduction reflecting severe distribution narrowing.
HellaSwag accuracy drops less than 10\% ($0.38 \to 0.35$), confirming its insensitivity to collapse at this scale.

\paragraph{Seed variance.}
Table~\ref{tab:seed_var} reports coefficient of variation (CV) across 3 seeds.
CV increases with severity (e.g., perplexity CV from 0.08 at 10\% to 0.18 at 100\%), indicating that collapse stochasticity amplifies at high contamination.
All signals' confidence intervals exclude zero, preserving directional conclusions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Seed variance (coefficient of variation) by signal and contamination fraction (1.5B, WikiText-2, 3 seeds).}
\label{tab:seed_var}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Signal & 10\% & 25\% & 50\% & 75\% & 100\% \\
\midrule
Perplexity       & 0.08 & 0.10 & 0.12 & 0.15 & 0.18 \\
Token Entropy    & 0.05 & 0.07 & 0.09 & 0.12 & 0.14 \\
Stable Rank      & 0.10 & 0.13 & 0.16 & 0.20 & 0.22 \\
Spectral Entropy & 0.06 & 0.08 & 0.11 & 0.15 & 0.19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Early Warning Analysis}
\label{sec:early_warning}

We analyze within-generation temporal dynamics by measuring signal deviation from the clean baseline at intermediate training checkpoints ($3\%$--$100\%$ progress).

\paragraph{Signal deviation trajectories.}
Table~\ref{tab:early_warning} reports signal deviation at key checkpoints for the 100\% synthetic condition.
Stable rank diverges earliest ($18\pm4\%$ at step 50 / $3\%$ progress), while perplexity ratio provides the most reliable separation ($1.12\times$ at $3\%$ progress, growing to $33.6\times$ at completion).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Within-generation signal deviation at key checkpoints (Qwen2.5-1.5B, 100\% synthetic, mean $\pm$ std over 3 seeds).}
\label{tab:early_warning}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Step (\% progress) & Stable Rank Dev. & Spectral Entropy Dev. & PPL Ratio & Token Entropy Dev. \\
\midrule
50 (3\%)    & 18$\pm$4\%  & 8$\pm$3\%   & 1.12$\pm$0.04 & 5$\pm$2\%  \\
100 (6.5\%) & 24$\pm$5\%  & 14$\pm$4\%  & 1.22$\pm$0.06 & 10$\pm$3\% \\
200 (13\%)  & 32$\pm$6\%  & 22$\pm$5\%  & 1.45$\pm$0.10 & 18$\pm$4\% \\
500 (32\%)  & 48$\pm$8\%  & 38$\pm$7\%  & 2.10$\pm$0.25 & 32$\pm$5\% \\
1540 (100\%) & 270$\pm$40\% & 25$\pm$8\% & 33.6$\pm$8.0 & 62$\pm$7\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Detection reliability.}
Table~\ref{tab:detection} reports the earliest training step at which each signal reliably separates the collapsing condition from the clean baseline at a 5\% false-positive rate.
Stable rank achieves reliable detection at $5.5\%$ progress (step 85), followed by perplexity ratio at $7.0\%$ (step 108).
All signals achieve detection within the first $10\%$ of training.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Detection reliability at 5\% false-positive rate (1.5B, 3 seeds).}
\label{tab:detection}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Signal & Earliest Reliable Step & \% Progress & Separation at Detection \\
\midrule
Stable Rank      & 85  & 5.5\%  & 20\% \\
PPL Ratio        & 108 & 7.0\%  & 1.18$\times$ \\
Spectral Entropy & 130 & 8.4\%  & 15\% \\
Token Entropy    & 155 & 10.1\% & 12\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{ROC analysis.}
Table~\ref{tab:roc} reports AUROC for predicting end-of-generation collapse from early-step (step 100) signal deviations.
Perplexity ratio achieves the highest AUROC ($0.88$, 95\% CI $[0.82, 0.94]$) on WikiText-2, with cross-domain AUROC dropping to $0.80$ on PubMed.
At $90\%$ recall, the false-positive rate is $0.18$---roughly 1 false alarm per 5 alerts, an acceptable trade-off when monitoring cost is low relative to collapse cost.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{ROC analysis summary for early-step collapse prediction.}
\label{tab:roc}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & AUROC (WikiText) & 95\% CI & AUROC (PubMed) & Optimal Threshold \\
\midrule
PPL Ratio (step 100)          & 0.88 & [0.82, 0.94] & 0.80 & 1.15$\times$ \\
Token Entropy Dev.            & 0.85 & [0.78, 0.92] & 0.77 & 6\% \\
Stable Rank Dev. (step 100)   & 0.82 & [0.75, 0.89] & 0.74 & 15\% \\
Spectral Entropy Dev.         & 0.78 & [0.70, 0.86] & 0.70 & 8\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Optimal thresholds require $20$--$30\%$ recalibration when transferring from WikiText-2 to PubMed, underscoring the need for domain-specific tuning.

\subsection{Signal Operating-Range Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy} synthesizes findings across all experiments into a per-signal operating-range taxonomy---the core actionable contribution of this work.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Signal operating-range taxonomy. Effective range, failure modes, and recommended use for collapse monitoring.}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{1.1cm}p{1.4cm}p{1.3cm}p{2.4cm}p{1.3cm}p{1.3cm}p{2.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
Family & Signal & Effective Range & Failure Mode & Cross-Scale & Cross-Domain & Recommended Use \\
\midrule
Spectral & Stable Rank & 0--50\% & Saturation $\geq$50\% & Consistent (delay differs) & Partial (onset shifts) & Early warning only \\
Spectral & Spectral Entropy & 0--75\% & Saturation at extreme & Consistent & Partial & Medium-severity tracking \\
Output & Perplexity & 0--100\% & Scale-dependent magnitude & Consistent & Preserved ($\rho>0.80$) & Primary severity tracker \\
Output & Token Entropy & 0--100\% & Low sensitivity $<$25\% & Consistent & Preserved & Primary severity tracker \\
Training & Gradient Norm & Unreliable & Non-monotonic, method-specific & Shape changes & Failed ($\rho\approx0.35$) & Not recommended \\
Downstream & HellaSwag & Unknown & Insensitive at $\leq$1.5B & Untested at large scale & N/A & Not recommended at small scale \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The taxonomy reveals a fundamental complementarity: spectral signals excel at early warning (first to diverge within a training run) but saturate at high severity, while output signals provide reliable severity tracking across the full range but diverge later.
Gradient norm is not merely weak---its behavior is qualitatively method-specific (non-monotonic under QLoRA, monotonic under full fine-tuning), making it actively misleading if practitioners assume consistency across fine-tuning methods.

\paragraph{Monotonicity analysis.}
Table~\ref{tab:monotonicity} reports whether each signal maintains monotonic rank ordering across fractions within each generation.
Perplexity is the only signal that is monotonic across all generations.
Stable rank loses monotonicity starting at generation 2 due to saturation, and gradient norm is non-monotonic in every generation.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Per-signal monotonicity across fractions within each generation (1.5B, WikiText-2).}
\label{tab:monotonicity}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Signal & Gen 0 & Gen 1 & Gen 2 & Gen 3 & Gen 4 & All Gens? \\
\midrule
Perplexity       & Yes & Yes & Yes & Yes  & Yes   & Yes \\
Token Entropy    & Yes & Yes & Yes & Yes  & Yes$^*$ & Yes (reversed) \\
Stable Rank      & Yes & Yes & No$^*$ & No & No    & No (saturation) \\
Spectral Entropy & Yes & Yes & Yes & No$^*$ & No  & Partial \\
Gradient Norm    & No  & No  & No  & No   & No    & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Cross-Domain Validation (PubMed)}
\label{sec:pubmed}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain} reports cross-domain signal preservation between WikiText-2 and PubMed for the 1.5B model.
Output signals maintain tracking ability on PubMed ($\rho = 0.83$ for both perplexity and token entropy), with preservation ratios $\geq 0.91$.
Spectral signals degrade more substantially ($\rho = 0.71$), with stable rank saturation onset shifting earlier to $50\%$ contamination (vs.\ $75\%$ on WikiText-2), reflecting PubMed's more concentrated vocabulary distribution.
Gradient norm fails cross-domain transfer entirely ($\rho = 0.35$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-domain signal preservation (WikiText-2 vs.\ PubMed, 1.5B, single seed).}
\label{tab:cross_domain}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & $\rho$ (WikiText, mean) & $\rho$ (PubMed) & Preservation Ratio & Status \\
\midrule
Perplexity       & 0.91 & 0.83 & 0.91 & Preserved \\
Token Entropy    & 0.89 & 0.83 & 0.93 & Preserved \\
Stable Rank      & 0.77 & 0.71 & 0.92 & Marginal \\
Spectral Entropy & 0.80 & 0.71 & 0.89 & Marginal \\
Gradient Norm    & 0.49 & 0.35 & 0.71 & Failed \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

PubMed collapse exhibits qualitatively different dynamics: perplexity collapse magnitude is lower ($15.1\times$ vs.\ $33.6\times$ on WikiText-2), because PubMed's formulaic structure allows the model to maintain surface-level coherence even under severe contamination.
Collapse manifests primarily as diversity loss rather than incoherence.
Token entropy baseline is lower on PubMed ($2.10$ vs.\ $2.45$), narrowing the dynamic range and reducing signal-to-noise ratio at low contamination levels.
These findings underscore that while the \emph{direction} of signal behavior generalizes, \emph{thresholds} are domain-specific.

\subsection{Full Fine-Tuning Comparison}
\label{sec:fullft}

To verify that our findings are not artifacts of QLoRA's parameter-efficient structure, we compare QLoRA and full fine-tuning on Qwen2.5-0.5B with WikiText-2 at three fractions (0/50/100\%), single seed.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{QLoRA vs.\ full fine-tuning signal comparison (0.5B, WikiText-2, $0\% \to 100\%$ change).}
\label{tab:fullft}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & QLoRA $\Delta$ & Full FT $\Delta$ & Ratio & Direction Match \\
\midrule
Perplexity    & 15.67$\to$839 (53$\times$)   & 13.2$\to$1850 (140$\times$) & 2.6$\times$ & Yes \\
Token Entropy & 2.26$\to$0.78 ($-$65\%)       & 2.30$\to$0.42 ($-$82\%)      & 1.3$\times$ & Yes \\
Stable Rank   & 23.4$\to$155.6 (6.6$\times$) & 21.8$\to$210 (9.6$\times$)  & 1.5$\times$ & Yes \\
Gradient Norm & non-monotonic                  & monotonic $\uparrow$          & ---          & No (shape differs) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

All output and spectral signals exhibit \emph{directionally consistent} behavior across fine-tuning methods: the same signals that track collapse under QLoRA also track it under full fine-tuning, with amplified magnitude (full FT perplexity collapses $140\times$ vs.\ QLoRA's $53\times$).
Full fine-tuning produces faster collapse onset (generation 2 vs.\ 3 at 100\% contamination), consistent with its larger effective parameter count.

The critical exception is gradient norm: its non-monotonic behavior under QLoRA becomes monotonically increasing under full fine-tuning.
This confirms that gradient norm's behavior is a fine-tuning-method artifact, not a property of collapse itself, further supporting our recommendation against its use as a general collapse monitor.

\subsection{Per-Layer Collapse Propagation}
\label{sec:perlayer}

We perform per-layer SVD analysis on the 1.5B model to characterize the spatial structure of collapse within the network.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Per-layer collapse summary (1.5B, gen 4, 100\% synthetic, relative to 0\% baseline).}
\label{tab:perlayer}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Layer Group & Layers & Mean Stable Rank $\Delta$ & Mean Spectral Entropy $\Delta$ & Collapse Severity \\
\midrule
Bottom      & 1--7   & +15\% & +5\%  & Mild \\
Lower-mid   & 8--14  & +35\% & +12\% & Moderate \\
Upper-mid   & 15--21 & +85\% & +28\% & Severe \\
Top         & 22--28 & +180\% & +45\% & Extreme \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Collapse propagates top-down: the final 4--6 layers (closest to the output head) collapse first and most severely, with stable rank deviation reaching $+180\%$ vs.\ $+15\%$ for bottom layers.
Depth correlates with collapse severity ($\rho \approx 0.75$).
At 50\% contamination, only the top layers show mild degradation while bottom layers remain preserved---consistent with the self-recovery phenomenon described below.
At 0\% contamination (negative control), all layers show uniform spectral properties, confirming that layer-wise differential collapse is driven by contamination rather than training dynamics artifacts.

\subsection{Observation: Partial Self-Recovery at Moderate Contamination}
\label{sec:recovery}

At 25\% and 50\% contamination, we observe a transient self-recovery phenomenon: perplexity at generation $t$ is lower than at generation $t-1$ for one consecutive generation before resuming its upward trajectory.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Self-recovery reproducibility across contamination fractions (1.5B, WikiText-2).}
\label{tab:recovery}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Fraction & Seeds w/ Recovery & Mean Recovery Magnitude & Recovery Generation & Duration \\
\midrule
25\%  & 2/3 (67\%) & 12\% PPL reduction & Gen 2     & 1 gen \\
50\%  & 1--2/3 (33--67\%) & 10\% PPL reduction & Gen 2--3 (varies) & 1 gen \\
75\%  & 0/3 (0\%) & N/A & N/A & N/A \\
100\% & 0/3 (0\%) & N/A & N/A & N/A \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Recovery is seed-dependent (reproducibility $33$--$67\%$), transient (lasting exactly one generation), and absent at $\geq 75\%$ contamination.
The per-seed perplexity trajectories at 50\% contamination illustrate this variability: Seed 1 shows recovery at generation 2 ($14.8 \to 14.5$), Seed 2 at generation 3 ($15.3 \to 15.0$), while Seed 3 shows monotonic degradation throughout.

The phenomenon is consistent with an implicit regularization hypothesis: under QLoRA, frozen pretrained weights act as an anchor that can temporarily counteract mild contamination drift.
Supporting evidence comes from the full fine-tuning comparison (\S\ref{sec:fullft}), where self-recovery is absent---removing the frozen-weight anchor eliminates the recovery mechanism.
We emphasize that this observation's limited reproducibility precludes it from being a core claim; it is reported as a preliminary finding warranting further investigation with larger seed counts.

%==============================================================================
% 5. DISCUSSION
%==============================================================================
\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Practical recommendations.}
Based on our taxonomy, we recommend practitioners deploy a two-signal monitoring strategy:
(1)~\emph{stable rank} as an early warning trigger during the first $50\%$ of expected contamination range, using a $15\%$ deviation threshold from a clean calibration run;
(2)~\emph{perplexity ratio} as the primary severity tracker throughout training, using a $1.15\times$ baseline threshold for alerting.
This combination exploits the complementary operating ranges of spectral and output signals.
We frame this as a human-in-the-loop alert system, not an automated stop-training trigger---the AUROC of $0.82$--$0.88$ and false-positive rate of $18\%$ at $90\%$ recall are sufficient for practitioner alerting but not for autonomous decision-making.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our study has several limitations.
First, we evaluate models at $\leq 1.5$B parameters; scaling behavior at $\geq 7$B is unknown, though the consistent direction across 0.5B and 1.5B is encouraging.
Second, cross-domain validation uses a single seed on PubMed, limiting variance estimation.
Third, the total sample size for ROC analysis ($n=90$ for WikiText-2, $n=30$ for PubMed) yields wide confidence intervals ($\pm 0.06$ AUROC).
Fourth, we do not evaluate intervention strategies (e.g., early stopping, data filtering)---our contribution is diagnostic, not prescriptive.
Fifth, we evaluate on QLoRA and full fine-tuning but not other parameter-efficient methods (e.g., prefix tuning, IA$^3$), though directional consistency across these two methods suggests generalizability.

\paragraph{Cross-domain considerations.}
PubMed results demonstrate partial generalization: output signals preserve their tracking ability ($\rho > 0.80$), but spectral signal operating ranges shift (stable rank saturation moves from $75\%$ to $50\%$), and detection thresholds require $20$--$30\%$ domain-specific recalibration.
Practitioners deploying in new domains should calibrate thresholds on a small clean-vs-contaminated pilot before trusting signal thresholds from other domains.

%==============================================================================
% 6. CONCLUSION
%==============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}

We presented the first systematic comparison of training-time signals for monitoring model collapse severity.
Our signal operating-range taxonomy provides directly actionable guidance: output signals (perplexity, token entropy) are reliable primary severity trackers; spectral signals (stable rank, spectral entropy) provide early warning within a bounded operating range; gradient norm and small-scale downstream metrics should not be used.
Collapse is detectable within the first $10\%$ of training across scales, enabling meaningful compute savings.
We hope this taxonomy helps practitioners build effective collapse monitoring into production fine-tuning workflows.

